{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And He said, for I will be with you</b>, etc. One needs to know what answer the supernal El is giving him with ״for I will be with you״ — for surely Moses knew that he was coming by His power, only he argued that given his own diminished worth he would not be adequate. Further it is difficult that He said <b>and this shall be the sign for you</b>, etc. — the sign is not known, and if it refers to the sign of the bush not being consumed as Rashi explained, the mind does not settle on this: was it really a doubt for Moses, heaven forbid, whether He could deliver? And further it is difficult what He concluded saying, <b>that I</b> etc., which implies that he was in doubt whether HaShem had sent him — and this is not so, for Moses said nothing that would express doubt in this matter." "<b>Now indeed</b>, the intent of the verse is along these lines: HaShem comes to answer him point by point. Corresponding to what you said, ״Who am I that I should go to Pharaoh?״ — know that there I will be with you, and from now on your greatness is greater than his greatness, and your crown is greater than his crown, and your awe is cast upon him more than his awe is cast upon you, and he ought to be in your eyes as a nobody. In this as well He contradicted him regarding what he said — that he was not worthy to bring them out — for HaShem showed him that his attainment is great. And His saying <b>and this shall be the sign for you</b> means: and this is the sign for you that you are worthy to do so great a thing as this — and what is the sign? ״That I have sent you״ — and had you not been worthy I would not have sent you, so what room is there for the concern that you are not worthy? And His saying <b>when you bring out</b> etc. intends to tell him that the mission will succeed in his hand to its completion, and he will bring the people out — he himself — and they will serve Elohim, etc.; and from now on the argument of ״and that I should bring out [the children of Israel]״ is nullified, for HaShem informs him the ultimate outcome of the matter, and there is no doubt when HaShem declares future things." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend in His saying <b>and this shall be for you</b> etc., along these lines: this thing — that you diminish yourself and say ״Who am I?״ — this is for you a sign that in the very midst of acquiring this trait [of humility], ״I have sent you.״ And His saying <b>when you bring out</b> etc., as we explained just above." "Further, it may intend along these lines: <b>and this shall be the sign for you that I</b> have decided the matter — that this mission shall be through you — <b>when you bring out</b> etc. <b>upon this mountain</b> — Sinai, in holiness — meaning the lowest of the mountains, and I do not choose the high mountains such as Tavor and Chermon. From this you learn that I desire only the lowly ones like you — the humble and lowly in your own eyes — and this lowly mountain. And all this is an answer to the argument of ״and that I should bring out״ etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:02.830966050Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}